---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  PeriodicTable as PeriodicTableContainer,
  ElementalNamesTree as ElementTree,
  ElementalNamesSingleChar as ArrayVisualizer,
  ElementalNamesAllPossibilities,
  ComplexityBenchmark,
  ElementSlotMachine,
  EricaBacktrackVisualizer,
  ElementalNameChecker,
} from "../../components/cave/nombres-elementales";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Elemental Names"
  description="Form names using the initials of chemical elements"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Among beakers and test tubes, two chemists are about to welcome their
        newborns. Everything is ready. Except the names. Each name must use
        chemical symbols. If it can't be broken down, it doesn't pass.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="fragment">
      <PeriodicTableContainer interactive={false} client:load />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <pre>
        - "I like Liam."
        — "Lithium. Americium. Perfect."
      </pre>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementSlotMachine
        client:load
        name="Liam"
        tokens={[{ symbol: "Li" }, { symbol: "Am" }]}
        playOn="revealVisible"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>"What about Nora?" — "Nitrogen. Oxygen. Radium. That works."</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementSlotMachine
        client:load
        name="Nora"
        tokens={[{ symbol: "N" }, { symbol: "O" }, { symbol: "Ra" }]}
        playOn="revealVisible"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>"Emma is a nice name too." — "Hmm... no. That doesn't work."</p>
      <p>
        They can't keep checking names by hand. They need an automated way to
        spot elemental names.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementSlotMachine
        client:load
        name="Emma"
        tokens={[null, null]}
        playOn="revealVisible"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <!-- Cheese Bytes Intro -->
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        My first thought was: this is trivial. We just scan the name, letter by
        letter. For each letter, we check if it matches an element in the
        periodic table. For example, let's try the name "Coco".
        <span>Coco</span>
        We could form it using: carbon, oxygen, carbon, oxygen.
      </p>
      <p>
        That seems to work. Some other names work too.
        <span>Whisky</span>
        So far, everything looks easy. But then we hit a problem. Take a name like
        "Erica". If we only take one letter at a time, there is no element with symbol
        "E". No "R". No "A". Can we say for sure that "Erica" is not elemental?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <ArrayVisualizer client:load />
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        In the periodic table, there are 14 single-letter elements and 104
        two-letter elements. None with three or more, that's also an important
        detail. If we look closely, we find Erbium and Calcium. These two
        elements, along with Iodine, allow us to form Erica.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <PeriodicTableContainer client:load />
  </section>
  <section>
    <div style="height: 14em">
      <ElementalNamesAllPossibilities initialText="Erica" showInput={false} />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        We now know Erica is elemental, but how can we validate this in an
        systematic way? We can start from the left and remove characters if they
        match an element, starting from single letter elements first and, if not
        possible, from two-letter elements.
      </p>
      <p>
        "E" does not exist as a chemical symbol. So we try two letters. "Er".
        That works. Erbium. So we move forward. Now we are left with "ica". "I"
        works. Iodine. We move forward again. Now we are left with "ca". "C"
        works. Carbon. We move forward. Now we are left with "a". "A" does not
        exist. So this path fails. And if we stop here, we conclude that "Erica"
        does not work. But that conclusion is wrong. This greedy implementation
        fails. The mistake happened earlier. When we matched "C", we had another
        valid option. Instead of taking "C", we could have taken "Ca". Calcium.
        And that would have finished the name.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem is not choosing one or two letters. The problem is that when
        more than one option works, we must try all of them. If we commit to the
        first one and never go back, we can miss a valid solution.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <EricaBacktrackVisualizer client:load playOn="revealVisible" />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Instead of committing to a single path, we can represent all
        possibilities at once. Each node represents a position in the name. From
        that position, we branch on every valid symbol. One branch takes one
        letter. Another branch takes two. Some branches fail. Others continue.
        As soon as one branch reaches the end, we know the name is elemental.
      </p>
      <p>
        Look at this point. From here, both choices are valid. C works. Ca also
        works. But choosing C leads to a dead end. Choosing Ca finishes the
        name. The problem is not that one option is wrong. The problem is that
        we chose too early. When more than one choice works, we must explore
        them all.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div style={{ height: "70vh" }}>
      <ElementTree showInput={true} initialText="Erica" client:load />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="code-naive-recursion" class="text-left text-sm">
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="typing"
      code={`ELEMENTS = {
    'h','he',
    'li','be','b','c','n','o','f','ne',
    'na','mg','al','si','p','s','cl','ar',
    'k','ca','sc','ti','v','cr','mn','fe','co','ni','cu','zn','ga','ge','as','se','br','kr',
    'rb','sr','y','zr','nb','mo','tc','ru','rh','pd','ag','cd','in','sn','sb','te','i','xe',
    'cs','ba','la','ce','pr','nd','pm','sm','eu','gd','tb','dy','ho','er','tm','yb','lu',
    'hf','ta','w','re','os','ir','pt','au','hg','tl','pb','bi','po','at','rn',
    'fr','ra','ac','th','pa','u','np','pu','am','cm','bk','cf','es','fm','md','no','lr',
    'rf','db','sg','bh','hs','mt','ds','rg','cn','fl','lv','ts','og'
  }

def is_elemental(name: str) -> bool:  # assume name is lowercase
    # STEP-1
    if name == "":
        return True

    # STEP-2
    if name[:1] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[1:]):
        return True

    # STEP-3
    if len(name) >= 2 and name[:2] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[2:]):
        return True

    # STEP-4
    return False`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Liam, Nora. They are elemental and it works fast. Emma is not elemental
        but is also fast. Coco, lovely name, is elemental. Is also pretty fast.
        How well this scales? Let's see. cococococo works fast, cococococo+A
        don't. Why?
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementalNameChecker client:visible initialValue="" debounceMs={200} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Some names like Liam do not branch that much, only one path is valid.
        Coco is the opposite, every step branches into two possibilities. This
        generates a wide tree of possibilities. If we have that many
        possibilities, why it takes no time to say it is elemental? The reason
        is that we are doing a depth-first search. We explore one path at a
        time, and as soon as we find a valid path, we stop. In the case of
        cococococo, the first path we try is the correct one. We never explore
        the other possibilities. But if we add an "a" at the end, we are forced
        to explore all paths, and that takes a lot of time. if insead of
        finishing in coa we add a ca, then, it's also fast because it's fast to
        find a valid path. The problem is not the number of possibilities. The
        problem is how many of them we need to explore before finding a valid
        one, if any.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some branches are repeated, they are the same. We are indeed solving the
        same problem multiple times. We need to be smarter. We can save the
        result of each position in the name. If we reach the same position
        again, we can reuse the saved result instead of recalculating it. This
        technique is called memoization, and it can make a huge difference in
        performance.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div style={{ height: "70vh" }}>
      <ElementTree showInput={true} initialText="Coco" client:load />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="code-final" class="text-left text-sm">
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="typing"
      code={`ELEMENTS = {
    'h','he',
    'li','be','b','c','n','o','f','ne',
    'na','mg','al','si','p','s','cl','ar',
    'k','ca','sc','ti','v','cr','mn','fe','co','ni','cu','zn','ga','ge','as','se','br','kr',
    'rb','sr','y','zr','nb','mo','tc','ru','rh','pd','ag','cd','in','sn','sb','te','i','xe',
    'cs','ba','la','ce','pr','nd','pm','sm','eu','gd','tb','dy','ho','er','tm','yb','lu',
    'hf','ta','w','re','os','ir','pt','au','hg','tl','pb','bi','po','at','rn',
    'fr','ra','ac','th','pa','u','np','pu','am','cm','bk','cf','es','fm','md','no','lr',
    'rf','db','sg','bh','hs','mt','ds','rg','cn','fl','lv','ts','og'
  }

# STEP-1
memo: dict[str, bool] = {}

# STEP-0
def is_elemental(name: str) -> bool:  # assume name is lowercase
    # STEP-2
    if name in memo:
        return memo[name]

    # STEP-0
    if name == "":
        return True

    if name[:1] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[1:]):
        # STEP-3
        memo[name] = True
        # STEP-0
        return True

    if len(name) >= 2 and name[:2] in ELEMENTS and is_elemental(name[2:]):
        # STEP-4
        memo[name] = True
        # STEP-0
        return True

    # STEP-5
    memo[name] = False
    # STEP-0
    return False`}
    />
  </section>

  <section style={{ height: "60vh" }}>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        With memoization, we avoid redundant calculations. Each position in the
        name is solved only once. This makes the algorithm much faster, even for
        names with many possibilities. The performance is no longer determined
        by the number of possibilities, but by the length of the name and the
        number of unique subproblems.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ElementalNameChecker
        client:visible
        initialValue=""
        debounceMs={200}
        showMemoToggle={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        DP can also solve this. There is an implementation detail that makes the
        complexity not linear, leave you that for you to discover and read you
        in the comments.
      </p>
      <p>
        This was todays cheese bytes. Remember, the code matters but the idea
        comes first.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <!-- Interactive demo -->
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
